     "title": "Reading Flawed Thinkers Without Flattening Them",
     "content": "Feynman is far from the only brilliant, historically significant figure whose personal conduct doesn't match the rigor of their public contributions -- this pattern recurs across the history of science, philosophy, and the arts. A useful, repeatable framework for engaging any such figure has three steps.\n\nFirst, separate the specific claim or method under discussion from the person's overall character. Evaluate the O-ring demonstration on its own evidence -- anyone can reason through that experiment -- rather than on how you feel about its author. Second, ask whether a given flaw is incidental to the achievement or embedded inside the method itself. Here, the flaw is about personal conduct, not about how he reasoned through physics or public safety, so the scientific work stands on its own regardless of the memoir's other chapters. That question matters because sometimes the flaw is embedded -- a method built on fabricated data, say -- and no amount of separating person from claim rescues it.\n\nThird, refuse both of the easy, lazy responses available: discarding an entire body of work because its author had real faults, or waving away real harm because the person was otherwise brilliant. Practice naming both things out loud whenever you cite a flawed source: this demonstration is a genuinely admirable piece of scientific reasoning, and this book also contains passages I'm not endorsing. That habit of dual acknowledgment, rather than a single verdict, is the actual transferable skill this lesson is teaching.",
